acters but these characters  don't talk to each other they don't know  each other they don't have any backstory  and if a network has a high question  coefficient that means and everyone  talks to everyone well I'm sure you can  imagine by now how it looks it's the  lowest for the prequels and then it goes  out in the other episodes so the  prequels were rubbish based on this as  well because there are just way too many  characters that don't talk to each other  and then in Episode seven it dropped  again so I can't quantify that for the  episode eight because I don't have the  screenplay yet and then I decided to  look at the most crucial question so who  is the most central in the Star Wars  universe so the most straightforward  measure is the degree where you  basically just look at the number of  neighbors so this guy is very central  because he knows six people and this guy  is less central because he knows only  three nodes so that's a very  straightforward thing and then I decided  to compare that with something called  betweenness and this measure tells you  how in between other characters someone  is so if I look at this guy and these  two other guys he's very crucial for  them because if they want to talk to  each other they have to go through the  green guy and if I take these two guys  the green guy is not that crucial for  them because they have other ways of  communicating as well so this basically  tells you how central a character is in  the story how much the character  compared which connects different  communities so this is how you compute  it mathematically that the crucial thing  is that you have to compute the shortest  paths between nodes and that's quite a  lot of work already  so the great thing in R is that there is  a package for everything so  I use the eye graft package and as I  said I was doing some of it in f-sharp  so basically I just called it from  f-sharp and we have a type provider for  that like the Jason type provider I was  showing you before  there is a type provider for our as well  so we just type our dots and you get  access to everything you have in your  our installation so that was easy and  this is how it looks for episode 7 so if  I just look at the degree the most  central character is Poe Dameron which  is one of the resistance pilots and then  you have all the other characters but  you can see that the main characters are  actually not there if you have seen  episode 7 if you haven't I'm sorry but  if you look at betweenness suddenly kylo  Ren is the second and Trey is the third  so this measure captures much more how a  character is crucial in the story how  the character connects different  communities so but PO is still the most  central character there he's celebrating  and now you might ask so who is the most  central of the whole network well before  episode 7 it was this guy obi-wan Kenobi  but then he died in episode 4 so he  probably can't continue being the most  central so when I actually included  episode 7 data Darth Vader still rules  the galaxy so as Jared mentioned  everyone wants to talk to me about Star  Wars and actually this is I did  normal sadistic PhD but this is my most  academically successful work to be  honest because I put all the networks in  adjacent format on my github with a  permissive Creative Commons license and  turns out people are using these  networks for education to teach people  network science as an example dataset  and then they have a higher headache  because they are trying to figure out  how to actually cite github or my blog  post in their academic papers and this  being  pretty successful and it was also  published in the actual paper magazine  in pro science which is a French version  of the Scientific American so I was  pretty excited about that  they sent me a paper copy and Wow  I did this while I was in Cambridge and  when I actually read it I don't speak  any French but I'm pretty sure this  doesn't say Cambridge so my takeaway  message for you is to like put your data  out because people will use them for  their own means they will get it wrong  but they will use them and then it was  useful for me as well because back then  I was doing cancer research and I was  reading a paper and I looked at some of  the figures there and they were trying  to predict oncogenes and tumor  suppressor genes and they were trying to  predict network between them and then  they had this figure there where they  claimed that tumor suppressors and  oncogenes have higher betweenness in  their networks and like betweenness I  know this from Star Wars so I think  there is a lot of value it's playing  with actual interpretable datasets  because sometimes you just run an  algorithm that you don't really  understand that while on your customer  database or on some gene database  something comes out and you don't really  understand the results but in case of  Star Wars you probably do so play with  interpretable data search please and if  you want to talk about Star Wars or  anything else just find me later or ping  me on Twitter or anything and made the  RB with you  you  you
 

